---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import release from '../../data/release.json';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Security"
  description="Residiuum security posture, supported versions statement, vulnerability reporting path, and threat-model links."
  path="/security/"
  eyebrow="Security"
  lead="Report vulnerabilities privately. Do not treat experimental software as production-hardened."
>
  <Callout title="Supported versions" variant="warning">
    <p>
      No production-supported release line is claimed at this time. Residiuum
      {release.productVersion} is experimental / early access. Security fixes
      land in the repository as the project iterates; there is no published LTS
      policy yet.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Vulnerability reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Prefer the repository’s GitHub security advisory flow when available. Do
    <strong>not</strong> submit vulnerability details through public issues.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <ExternalLink href={release.githubSecurity}>
        GitHub security page for frogfishio/dingodb
      </ExternalLink>
    </li>
    <li>
      If a private reporting route is not yet configured on GitHub, open a
      minimal public issue that asks for a private contact channel—without
      including exploit details—or contact maintainers through the project’s
      published channels when they exist.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Callout title="Do not invent SLAs">
    <p>
      This website does not publish a response SLA or a security email address
      that is not confirmed in the repository. When the project publishes a
      security policy file, that document is authoritative.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Dependency and release posture</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rust workspace dependencies are reviewed through normal PR process and <code>deny.toml</code> policy in-repo.</li>
    <li>Release packaging gates are documented under <code>doc/reference/operations/RELEASE_ARTIFACTS.md</code>.</li>
    <li>Signed release artifact policy is “not yet published” until maintainers confirm it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Threat model</h2>
  <p>
    A draft in-tree threat model exists for early security work. It is not a
    substitute for independent review before production use.
  </p>
  <p>
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/doc/wip/security/THREAT_MODEL.md'}>
      doc/wip/security/THREAT_MODEL.md
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Known security-adjacent limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Continuation-token authentication (DEF-097) remains open—do not claim
      authenticated continuation tokens.
    </li>
    <li>
      Network multi-node remains experimental and is not a production security
      boundary claim.
    </li>
    <li>
      Formal models (for example Heap TLA+) are design/evidence work; they are
      not implementation proofs of the full product surface.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <ExternalLink href={release.github} class="btn btn-primary">
      View source
    </ExternalLink>
    <a href="/status/" class="btn btn-secondary">Status and limitations</a>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
